  "query_preview": ".. /Tuesday through Sunday/ \nUpper level pattern is rather dynamic through the long range. It \nstarts out with a broad ridge of high pressure situated over the \nGulf of Alaska while a sharp trough is at around 180 longitude. \nThat trough will be advancing east through the start of the long \nrange period and will proceed to push the ridge east into Canada \nby Thursday. Behind it more zonal flow takes over, but not for \nlong. An upper level low will be digging SE from Russia by the \nweekend and will eventually anchor a broad trough stretching from \nthe Bering Sea to the Panhandle by Sunday.\n\nThis means that the forecast for the panhandle is rather dynamic \nas well. It will start out rather quiet with the ridge over the \ngulf initially keeping most precipitation at bay. However, that \nridge will be starting to flatten enough that flow turns more \nonshore over the northern panhandle and precipitation starts to \nreturn to the forecast. The rain is not expected to start in \nernest until Wednesday as a front works its way eastward across \nthe gulf. This front does have some tropical connections, but it \nis moving eastward at a decent clip and looks like it will be \nweakening as it goes. So while there may be periods of moderate or\nheavy rain rates (especially for the NE Gulf coast), the rapid \neastward movement and weakening nature means that those rain rates\nwill not be over any particular area for long. Expected rain \ntotals for Wed look to be around 1 to 3 inches mainly from the \ncentral panhandle Northwestward with the highest totals expected \nalong the NE gulf coast. This system will also bring some wind \nwith 25 to 30 kt SW winds possible in the gulf while the northern \ninner channels will see a burst of southerly winds up to 30 kt \nduring the day Wednesday.",
